\f4\fs26 \'93What? I am not the mother and he is not the father. How can this be?\'94 Sarah was trying to comprehend what she was hearing, but this was really testing the limits of credulity.\
\'93You and Cracker have no DNA in common with the child. According to the data base we have records of many people, male and female, that possess parts of the DNA sequence. I can superimpose the strands over one another and you can see the points of similarity. It seems that many of them have been dead for years and those guys possess more DNA in common with the child than the ones still alive. I think that should be the other way around. At a guess I would say that they are all distantly related and have a common ancestor a few generations back. I will have to do some manual research, maybe even look in some books. This is exciting!  See, look, I can construct almost the entirety of the helix from the common genes of our list of candidates here. There is just a little bit that is different, but I think there are more than sufficient points of commonality to locate the ancestor. I can reconstruct a family tree using the genetic information.\'94\
\'93Can\'92t you chuck the names through Ancestor-Link? Cracker joked.\
\'93We do use those resources when absolutely necessary, but prefer to rely on the genetic information. Family trees lie, science does not. On this occasion, however, it\'92s a great idea my friend. An extra patch for you tonight. Okay let me get these names on the link and see what turns up.\'94\
